For RPC_CLIENT_LOCAL (RPC_MODE=server), the "client" and "server" share the
process, but pkcs11-top-level code only calls hal_rpc_client_init() from
C_Initialize -- never hal_rpc_server_init(). The original LOCAL-mode
hal_rpc_client_init was a bare `return HAL_OK`, so the keystore was never
initialized, ks_token_init() never ran, the seed-pin patch never applied,
and every login failed (PBKDF2 compare against zeroed stored pins).

For LOCAL mode, fold the keystore-initialization half of
hal_rpc_server_init() into hal_rpc_client_init(). The transport half is
unnecessary in LOCAL mode (dispatch tables are direct calls, no socket).

diff --git a/rpc_client.c b/rpc_client.c
--- a/rpc_client.c
+++ b/rpc_client.c
@@ -1194,6 +1194,13 @@ const hal_rpc_pkey_dispatch_t * hal_rpc_pkey_dispatch = &hal_rpc_mixed_pkey_disp
 hal_error_t hal_rpc_client_init(void)
 {
 #if RPC_CLIENT == RPC_CLIENT_LOCAL
+  /* Keystore init is the seed-pin load-bearing step: ks_token_init() runs
+     the seed_pin_from_plaintext() patch on a fresh token. hal_rpc_server_init()
+     is never called by our PKCS#11 consumer, so we must do it here. */
+  hal_error_t err;
+  if ((err = hal_ks_init(hal_ks_volatile, 1)) != HAL_OK ||
+      (err = hal_ks_init(hal_ks_token, 1))    != HAL_OK)
+    return err;
   return HAL_OK;
 #else
   return hal_rpc_client_transport_init();
